./skills/hopper/SKILL.md:28:2. **`.hopper/` exists but `.hopper/AGENTS.md` has no `## Approved Vendors` section** — this is a pre-v0.40.0 project that predates the vendor whitelist gate; every dispatch (including an explicit `--vendor` override) will be refused with `E_APPROVED_VENDORS_SECTION_MISSING`. Point the user at the repository's `MIGRATION.md` for the fix. Deciding which vendors to approve is a security-relevant whitelist decision — Safety Rules below already forbids editing `.hopper/AGENTS.md` without the user explicitly asking for that edit, and this case is no exception: **ask the user which vendors to approve, then make the edit they confirm.** Do not guess a vendor list and do not silently skip the task waiting on this — surface the blocker.
./commands/dispatch.md:49:- **What "host≠vendor enforced" actually means here (fixed 2026-07-31 — see CHANGELOG 0.39.0)**: this Claude Code session is detected as the `claude-code` host from Claude-Code-specific environment markers (no wrapper script sets it, unlike the Tier-C hosts), and the comparison is by FAMILY (`claude`/`claude-code` → anthropic; `codex` → openai; `grok` → xai; `kimi` → moonshot), not raw string equality — this is what makes the `claude-code`/`claude` naming mismatch catchable at all. `copilot`/`opencode`/`mimo`/`agy` have NO family mapping (each is a genuinely multi-backend router across model companies, not guessable), so dispatching to any of those is never blocked by this guard regardless of host. And if this session's host identity can't be confidently determined at all, the guard does not silently allow the dispatch through unremarked — `hopper-dispatch` prints a `notice:` line saying the isomorphism check did not run, so a swallowed check is always visible in the output, not invisible.
