./hosts/claude-code/README.md:93:Expected: a `hopper standalone (CLI v0.8.1)` banner. If you see it, **Prong 1 PASSES** — record this in `.hopper/HOPPER-FEEDBACK.md` per the unified user-action gate (spec §11).
./hosts/claude-code/README.md:175:> **`claude` as a VENDOR (v0.9.0+).** Distinct from the above: there is now a `claude` *vendor* adapter, so a hopper running under a DIFFERENT host (codex / opencode / grok / standalone CLI) can dispatch a task TO `claude -p`. The host≠vendor guard (`validateHostVendorSeparation`) is meant to block the only nonsensical case — a Claude Code host dispatching back to the `claude` vendor (self-dispatch) — and **as of v0.39.0 it actually does, for this host**. Billing for `claude -p` against a Claude plan churned through 2026 (the 2026-06-15 separate-Agent-SDK-credit split was later rolled back), so the adapter is billing-agnostic — verify the live policy at anthropic.com if cost matters. See `cli/src/vendors/claude.js` for the isolation/permission knobs (`HOPPER_CLAUDE_BARE=1` for deterministic CI isolation, `HOPPER_CLAUDE_PERMISSION_MODE` to override the permission mode).
./hosts/claude-code/README.md:177:> **How the guard works for THIS host, specifically (fixed 2026-07-31 — see CHANGELOG 0.39.0, `cli/src/host-detect.js` + `VENDOR_FAMILY` in `cli/src/validation.js`).** Before the fix, this guard was a silent no-op for Claude Code on two counts: (1) `hostVendor` came only from `HOPPER_HOST_VENDOR`, which only the Tier-C bash wrappers set — `hosts/claude-code/bin` does not exist, so nothing ever set it here; and (2) even with a host identity, the comparison was raw string equality, and the natural Claude Code host id (`claude-code`) never equals the vendor name (`claude`) as a string. Both are now fixed: a Claude Code session is self-detected from Claude-Code-specific environment markers (`CLAUDECODE`, `CLAUDE_CODE_ENTRYPOINT`, `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID`) when `HOPPER_HOST_VENDOR` is unset, and the comparison is by FAMILY (`claude`/`claude-code` → `anthropic`), which bridges the naming mismatch. This means `--vendor claude` (or an AGENTS.md default that resolves to `claude`) is now actually rejected from this host. It also means the fix is scoped: `copilot`/`opencode`/`mimo`/`agy` have no family mapping (each spans multiple backend model companies depending on account/config, so a single family would be a guess, not a fact) and are therefore NOT blocked by this guard even in a same-product self-dispatch shape. And if the self-detection cannot confidently identify the host at all, the dispatcher does not silently let the request through — it prints an explicit `notice:` line saying the isomorphism check did not run, rather than staying quiet about it.
